  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\n- Dry and mild weather to continue through Sunday\n\nHigh pressure continues to dominate the weather over the region as \nit has for the past few days. The upper ridge axis remains to our \nwest, allowing for plenty of subsidence to keep even cloud cover \nlimited across the area. \n\nWe do see one small caveat in the clear and dry weather, with little \nto no impact expected. There is an upper trough across Canada that \nspills over the ridge and drops down over the Lower Great Lakes \nWednesday night into Thursday. A weak front drops through our area \nat that time. There is very little moisture associated with this \nfront, as the flow will be from NE Canada initially, and then the \nWisconsin area. Neither of these will advect any moisture in, so a \ndry frontal passage is expected. \n\nThe only thing this frontal passage will do is drop temperatures a \ncouple of degrees temporarily. 850 mb temperatures drop from +9C on \nWednesday, to +6 to +7C on Thursday. This will take max temps from \naround 70F on Wed, to mid 60s Thu and Fri. Temperatures will then \nrecover a bit for Saturday through Monday as the low level flow \nbecomes from the SW again. Highs will approach 70F once again on \nSunday before clouds and rain chances appear on Monday.\n\n- Rain chances arrive Monday; Pattern change starting Tuesday\n\nAs mentioned above, a front will start to approach the area on \nMonday. This occurs as a strong upper low moves across Canada. The \nfront initially slows down as it becomes parallel to the upper flow \nwith the low so far north, and due to how much the strong ridge has \nto be flattened.\n\nIt will take another strong upper trough diving in from the NW to \npush the front through around Tuesday of next week. This does not \nlook to be a major rain producer for the area as it will once again \nnot have a good moisture source to pull from. \n\nWhat the front will do however is bring much cooler air down, with \nthe upper wave coming in from NW Canada. Models are showing 850 mb \ntemperatures dropping down to below zero in the wake of this system. \nDefinitely much cooler than this week.",
